    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i've been trying out this box breathing thing (4 in, 4 hold, 4 out, 4 hold) to help with stress but i wanted to know the actual science behind it. like how does that specific timing actually affect my nervous system or cortisol?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The science behind box breathing, or square breathing, is fascinating, as it directly engages the parasympathetic nervous system, which is the body's \"rest and digest\" mode. When you breathe in for a count of four, hold, exhale for four, and hold again, you are essentially sending a signal to your vagus nerve that tells your body it's safe. This regulated, rhythmic pattern helps to counteract the \"fight or flight\" response, which is what spikes cortisol levels when you're stressed. Over time, consistent practice can help lower baseline cortisol and promote a calmer state.",
      "source": "target",
